{%- set churchIntro -%}<p class="page-intro">All Saints' crowns the hill at Church Lench, a few miles north-west of Harvington, and looks out over the countryside towards the Malverns. It is the C in the ARCH Benefice, so it shares its rector and its Sunday rota with St James in Harvington. The church is listed Grade II*.</p>{%- endset -%}
{%- set churchAbout -%}<h2>The church through the centuries</h2>
    <p>Church Lench belonged to Evesham Abbey from 708, and Domesday Book records a priest here in 1086. The nave of today's church was built in the 12th century, and its blocked north doorway is from that time. Over the next three centuries a south aisle, a row of high clerestory windows and finally the battlemented west tower were added.</p>
    <p>Frederick Preedy, who restored St James in Harvington, restored All Saints' first, in the 1850s: he rebuilt the chancel and porch, added a vestry and organ, and filled most of the windows with some of his earliest stained glass, made between 1854 and 1867.</p>
    <h2>Things to see</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Six bells, rehung in 2000. The oldest, dated 1600, is inscribed "GEVEF THANKS TO GOD"; the rest came from Taylor of Loughborough between 1869 and 1876.</li>
      <li>Preedy's windows, especially the east window over the altar.</li>
      <li>A board listing the rectors of Church Lench from 1269.</li>
    </ul>{%- endset -%}
